\begin_layout Title
Eliciting Individual Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 22, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Like the week before, the set of readings this week focused on individual
 preferences.
 Two of the papers, Hoffman 
\emph on
et al
\emph default
.
 (1994) and List (2007), were concerned with the notion of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The three remaining papers, Holt and Laury (2002); Dave, Eckel, and Johnson
 (2010); and Andreoni and Sprenger (2012) conducted experiments in order
 to elicit risk preferences and estimate risk aversion parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Previous research on ulitmatum and dictator games have found that first
 movers in such games tend to offer more than noncooperative game theory
 predicts.
 Hoffman 
\emph on
et al.
 
\emph default
(1994) researched different variations of these games and found that when
 the right to be the first mover is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

earned,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then first movers behave in a more self-regarding manner.
 In addition, self-regarding offers increased in dictator double blind experimen
ts.
 Moreover, List (2007) found that when the action set allows for the dictator
 to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

take
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 money from the other player, fewer agents are willing to transfer money.
 In this setup, the first movers may believe that by simply electing not
 to take money is a form of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 These experiments raise fundamental questions regarding the nature and
 origins of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Do people actually behave in a fair and caring manner, or are they more
 concerned with how others perceive them? Are they truly giving out of the
 goodness of their heart, or are they just attempting to satisfy some well-estab
lished social norm? Further research is needed in order to test whether
 or not honesty and sharing are best, rational policies in close-knit communitie
s.
 Do we still observe altruistic and fair behavior when no prior social relations
hip has been established?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The three other papers mentioned attempted to measure an agent's preference
 for risk and the risk aversion parameter associated with his/her utility
 function.
 Holt and Laury (2002) wanted to measure the degree of risk aversion over
 a wide range of payoffs.
 Depending on the level of the stakes, they believed a utility function
 exhibiting increasing relative risk aversion, but decreasing absolute risk
 aversion may offer better predicitons.
 Dave, Eckel, and Johnson (2010) estimated the risk aversion parameter for
 different categories of subjects using two distinct elicitation methods.
 They found that using the simpler method to elicit risk preferences for
 individuals with low math skills providing better predictions than the
 more complicated method.
 Finally, Andreoni and Sprenger (2012) estimated time preferences from convext
 budget sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In regards to risk aversion, many of the papers I have read do not address
 the fact that risk preferences may change depending on the situation.
 While risk elicitation methods may be able to determine my general preferences
 and tendencies towards risk, they are unable to account for every possible
 scenario or change.
 In fact, many psychological studies have shown that people are more risky
 when they have an optimistic demeanor, as opposed to a pessimisstic one.
 Individuals with a more negative outlook on life tend to exhibit higher
 levels of risk aversion.
 Furthermore, an agent's preferences towards risk will change depending
 on the situation or the choices available.
 How can we can account for these ever changing attitudes toward risk?
\end_layout

